course: everyday
lesson: 305
title: Catch and release, practising well and sharing sports gear
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fish is covered in a thin layer of slime that protects it from germs, like a coat. Dry hands rub this slime off, so people who release fish wet their hands first.
explain: Getting better at anything takes practice, and practice can be frustrating. Breaking a hard skill into small steps makes each step possible, and the small steps add up.
explain: Example: a boy in Ontario who cannot do a full cartwheel practises just putting his hands down, then kicking one leg up, then both. In two weeks he does the whole cartwheel.
explain: Being on a team means others count on you: if you will miss practice, tell the coach in advance so they can plan.
explain: Shared helmets and gloves can pass on germs and head lice, so clean them between users.
fact: Before touching a fish you will release, wet your hands so you do not rub off its protective slime coat.
fact: The slime coat protects a fish from germs; damage to it can make the fish ill.
fact: Return a released fish gently to the water, holding it until it swims away by itself.
fact: A fish hook is sharp, so let an adult help take it out while you hold the fish gently.
fact: When practice is frustrating, break the skill into small steps and practise one at a time.
fact: Small steps practised often lead to big improvement over time.
fact: If you will miss practice, tell the coach in advance, because the team plans around who comes.
fact: Shared helmets, gloves and pads should be cleaned between users.
fact: Shared helmets and hats can spread head lice (tiny insects that live in hair; their eggs are called nits) and germs from one person to another.
exercise: A girl in Minnesota catches a fish she wants to release. What should she do before touching it? | Wet her hands | Dry her hands on a towel | Put on woolly gloves | Rub sand on her hands
exercise: Why do people wet their hands before handling a fish they will release? | Dry hands rub off the fish's protective slime coat | Wet hands make the fish less slippery, so it is much easier to hold still | Fish like the feeling of warm water and stay calm | It keeps the fish from biting
exercise: What does the slime on a fish do? | It protects the fish from germs | It helps the fish see in the dark | It makes the fish heavier so it sinks | It is only dirt from the river bed
exercise: How should a caught fish be returned to the water? | Gently, held in the water until it swims away | Thrown as far out into the deep water as possible, so it cannot be caught again | Dropped from the top of the bank | Left on the grass until it flips back
exercise: A boy keeps failing to dribble a basketball between his legs and gets angry. What helps most? | Break it into small steps and practise each one | Give up because he is not good at basketball | Try the whole move faster and harder | Only practise when the coach is watching
exercise: Why does breaking a hard skill into small steps help? | Each step is possible, and the small steps add up to the whole skill | Small steps mean you never have to try the hard part of the skill at all | Coaches only allow small steps in practice | Small steps make the skill easier to forget
exercise: A girl in Brisbane knows she will miss netball practice on Thursday for a dentist visit. What should she do? | Tell the coach before Thursday | Just not turn up and explain later | Ask a friend to pretend to be her | Say nothing because one practice does not matter
exercise: Why should you tell the coach in advance when you will miss practice? | The team plans the session around who is there | The coach wants to give you extra homework to make up for the missed session | You will be banned if you do not | The coach must phone the dentist
exercise: At a bike club, children share helmets from a box. What should happen between users? | The helmets are cleaned | The helmets are shaken out | The straps are made tighter | Nothing, helmets cannot spread anything
exercise: What can spread through shared helmets that are not cleaned? | Head lice and germs | Broken bones | Asthma | Short sight
exercise: A boy is frustrated after an hour trying to learn to juggle three balls. What is a good plan? | Practise with one ball, then two, then three | Keep throwing all three balls as high and as fast as he possibly can | Stop for good because juggling is too hard | Use heavier balls so they fall slower
exercise: A team's goalkeeper gloves are passed from player to player. Why clean them? | So germs are not passed from hand to hand | So the gloves stay soft and the next player catches better | So they become waterproof | So the other team cannot use them
test: A boy in Wales catches a trout with dry hands and squeezes it hard before letting it go. Why might the fish get ill later? | Its protective slime coat was rubbed off and germs can get in | It has been out of the water so long that it has forgotten how to swim | It ate too much bait on the hook | The cold water will be a shock after the warm air, and it will freeze
test: Which is true about getting better at a skill? | Small steps practised often add up to big improvement | You either have talent or you never improve | Practising once a year is enough | Getting frustrated means you should stop
test: The coach plans a practice match with exactly ten players, and one player does not come and does not say so. What is the problem? | The coach planned around him and now the teams are wrong | The coach will now have to put on gloves and play in goal himself for the rest of the season | The match must be cancelled for a year | Nothing, coaches never plan who comes
test: A girl finds a nit in her hair a week after borrowing a friend's cycling helmet. What is the likely link? | Head lice can spread through shared helmets | Helmets make lice grow from sweat | The lice came from the bike tyres | Cycling in the wind gives you lice
test: You want to learn a hard piece of piano music and you feel like giving up. What is the best plan? | Learn a few bars at a time, then join them up | Play the whole piece again and again at full speed | Wait until you feel ready to play it all perfectly | Choose only easy pieces from now on
test: Which is the kindest way to release a fish? | Wet hands, a gentle hold, and back into the water quickly | Dry hands and a firm squeeze around the middle, so it cannot slip and fall | A photo session of ten minutes on the bank | Dropping it into the water from a bridge
test: Why should a team's shared shin pads be wiped down after use? | So sweat and germs are not passed on to the next player | So they look new and shiny for the team photos at the end of the season | So they fit the next player better | So the coach can count them
test: A boy cannot do a handstand and keeps falling. Which is the best next step? | Practise kicking up against a wall a little each day | Try only on hard ground so he learns quickly | Give up, because handstands are only for gymnasts who started as babies | Try once a month and hope it works
test: caught a fish!! letting it go, can i grab it with dry hands | Wet your hands first to protect its slime coat | Yes, dry hands grip it better | Wear thick gloves instead | Wipe the slime off first
test: cant do the kick right, so annoying, gonna quit | Break it into small steps and practise one at a time | Quit, some people just can't learn | Do the whole kick 100 times angry | Only practise when the coach is watching, coz there's no point otherwise
test: cant make training sat, do i tell coach? | Yes, tell the coach in advance | No, just don't turn up | Tell a teammate on the day | Only tell him after the game
test: borrowing a helmet from the club box, just put it on? | Clean it first, shared helmets spread lice and germs | Fine, helmets can't carry anything, they're just plastic and foam | Fine if it fits | Wear it backwards to be safe
explain: A boy in Canada wore a helmet at practice that another child with head lice had used; a week later the whole family had lice.
explain: A girl in Ireland gave up the piano after one hard week; a year later she wished she had kept going, because her friend who practised a little every day now played well.
fact: Head lice and germs can spread through shared helmets, hats and hairbrushes.
fact: Most people improve slowly, and giving up at the hardest point means losing what they have already learned.
test: can i borrow your helmet, mine's at home | Clean it first, shared helmets can spread lice | Yes, just put it on | No, never use another helmet | Yes, wear it backwards
test: ugh i suck at this, gonna quit | Break it into small steps and practise often | Quit, some people just aren't born sporty and you'll never get it | Practise once a month | Only practise when it's easy
test: caught a fish, gonna squeeze it to get the hook out | Wet hands, hold it gently, let an adult help | Squeeze hard so it can't move | Throw it on the grass first | Hold it by the eyes
test: did u hear a kid shared his mate's helmet at hockey and "got nits so bad they had to shave his head". lesson? | Clean shared helmets between users; they can spread lice | Never wear a helmet at all | Nits only come from dirty people, so he must never wash his hair | Shaving is the only cure for nits
test: my uncle says if u touch a fish with dry hands "it dies instantly". what's prob exaggerated | Dying instantly; dry hands damage the slime coat, which can make it ill | That fish have a slime coat that helps keep germs out of their skin | That wet hands are better | That fish live in water
test: kids say the boy who quit swimming "is a quitter, always gives up". pass it on? | No, it's unkind gossip; break hard skills into small steps instead | Yes, he's a quitter, and quitters always quit everything they try | Yes, and tell his coach | Yes, it's true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct handling of a fish that is to be released? | The hands shall be wetted to protect its slime coat | The fish shall be held tightly with dry hands, so that it does not escape | The fish shall be thrown back from a height | The fish shall be left on land briefly to rest
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a player who will miss practice informs the coach in advance. | The team plans around who will attend | The coach must record absences for payment | Absence is otherwise unlawful | The coach will then cancel the practice
exam: It is given that practice of a skill has become frustrating. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The skill shall be broken into small steps, practised one at a time | The skill shall be abandoned entirely | The skill shall be practised only at full speed | The skill shall be attempted once a month only, in order that frustration be avoided entirely
exam: State the risk associated with sharing helmets, hats and hairbrushes. | They may spread head lice and germs | They may change the colour of one's hair | They may cause the helmet to shrink | They carry no risk whatsoever
